      <!-- SECTION 5 -->
      <section id="solvent-free">
        <h2>Solvent-Free and Water-Based Contact Adhesive Technologies</h2>
        <p>
          Regulatory and occupational health pressure on solvent-borne contact adhesives has intensified progressively since the EU Solvents Directive (1999/13/EC) and the subsequent VOC Directive (2004/42/EC) imposed emission limits on coating and adhesive manufacturing facilities. In shoe manufacturing — the single largest application of solvent-borne neoprene contact adhesives globally — the occupational exposure to MEK and toluene in manual application cabinets has driven adoption of solvent-free alternatives by compliance-driven brands, particularly in Europe and the United States. Three principal technology platforms address this transition.
        </p>
        <p>
          Water-based polychloroprene dispersions use emulsion polymerisation chemistry to produce a colloidal dispersion of polychloroprene particles in water, stabilised by anionic or non-ionic surfactants. The adhesive mechanism is unchanged — film formation occurs as water evaporates, the particles coalesce, and the resulting polychloroprene film undergoes strain-induced crystallisation on contact bonding. However, water-based systems have inherently longer open times (10–30 minutes compared to 5–15 minutes for solvent-borne), require higher minimum film formation temperatures (typically above 15°C), and are sensitive to temperature and humidity during application — limitations that constrain their use in cool or outdoor environments. High-viscosity solvent-free neoprene pastes overcome the open-time and temperature sensitivity issues by eliminating the carrier entirely: a high molecular weight polychloroprene resin is blended with tackifying resin, filler, and antioxidant in a paste format applied by roller or brush. Zero VOC, zero flammability risk, and excellent immediate bond strength make these the premium solution for enclosed manufacturing environments with strict emission controls. Our article on <a href="/volatile-organic-compounds-and-pollution" target="_top">VOC emissions and industrial pollution</a> covers the regulatory framework that is driving this transition across all solvent-borne adhesive categories.
        </p>
        <div class="callout callout-amber">
          <strong>Rule of Thumb</strong>
          Water-based contact adhesives require substrate temperature above 15°C and relative humidity below 80% for reliable film formation. Applying water-based neoprene adhesive to cold substrates (below 10°C) or in high humidity delays coalescence, producing a weak, powdery film that fails adhesively rather than cohesively — the most common failure mode in cold-climate flooring installation.
        </div>
      </section>

