      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section id="applications">
        <h2>Industrial Applications and Critical Surface Preparation</h2>
        <p>
          Contact adhesives serve several of the highest-volume industrial bonding applications globally. Shoe manufacturing is the pre-eminent market — the bonding of rubber and thermoplastic rubber (TPR) soles to leather, synthetic leather, or textile uppers is almost universally accomplished with neoprene contact adhesive, typically the two-component MDI-crosslinked grade for performance footwear. Furniture manufacturing is the second major application: the lamination of high-pressure laminate (HPL) decorative surfaces to particle board or MDF substrates in kitchen worktops, cabinet doors, and furniture panels requires a contact adhesive capable of strong, uniform bonding across large areas. Automotive interior assembly — headliner fabric to moulded fibre board, door panel fabric lamination, trim component bonding — uses both neoprene and SBS systems depending on the temperature and peel performance requirements.
        </p>
        <p>
          In construction, SBS contact adhesives are used extensively for floor covering installation (vinyl, rubber, linoleum adhesion to subfloors), bonding of insulation panels, and general laminate attachment. The competitive pressure from polyurethane adhesive systems — particularly one-component moisture-cure PU systems covered in our article on <a href="/polyurethane-sealants-chemistry-cure-weatherproofing" target="_top">polyurethane sealants and chemistry</a> — is most acute in construction panel bonding, where PU offers structural-level bond strength and gap-filling capability that contact adhesives do not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Surface preparation is the single most significant determinant of contact adhesive performance, and the most frequently neglected step in industrial application. Both surfaces must be clean, dry, and free from release agents, lubricants, dust, and fingerprint contamination. Porous surfaces (foam, leather, fabric, MDF) absorb the adhesive efficiently — a single coat applied by brush or spray typically provides adequate coverage and tack. Non-porous surfaces (metal, glass, HPL laminate, rigid plastics) require very uniform, thin coats; heavy application on non-porous surfaces results in trapped solvent and reduced tack. Plastics with low surface energy — polypropylene, polyethylene, PTFE — require corona treatment, flame treatment, or a bonding primer before contact adhesive application; without surface activation, adhesion will be consistently weak regardless of adhesive selection or open time management.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How does a contact adhesive create an instant bond on pressing?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Contact adhesives form instant bonds through auto-adhesion (polymer chain interdiffusion across the interface under pressure) and, in neoprene systems, strain-induced crystallisation. Mechanical pressing causes polychloroprene chains to align from random coils into ordered crystalline domains — these crystalline structures form within seconds and provide high cohesive strength, generating the immediate peel resistance that defines the contact bond. SBS systems rely on physical entanglement and tackifying resin interaction rather than crystallisation, giving good but lower immediate tack than neoprene. The crystallisation in neoprene is thermoreversible — heat allows repositioning before the bond is permanent.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the difference between open time and closed assembly time in contact adhesives?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Open time is the period after application during which the solvent evaporates and the film reaches the dry-tacky state required for bonding. For solvent-borne neoprene, this is typically 5–20 minutes depending on solvent blend, coat weight, and ambient conditions. Closed assembly time is the brief window after pressing during which repositioning is possible — in true contact adhesives this is very short (under one minute). Managing open time is the most common application error: bonding before the film is dry enough traps solvent and reduces bond strength; bonding after the film has over-dried reduces auto-adhesion and peel performance. Touch testing — film should feel dry but remain slightly tacky — is the standard workshop check.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why is polychloroprene used in contact adhesives rather than other rubbers?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Polychloroprene's unique capacity for strain-induced crystallisation generates high, immediate peel strength on contact bonding — a property not shared by SBR or most other synthetic rubbers. Additionally, neoprene offers excellent ozone and UV resistance, inherent flame retardancy from the chlorine substituent, a broad temperature service range (−30°C to +80°C), and good compatibility with a wide range of substrates. These properties combined make polychloroprene the premium base polymer for demanding contact adhesive applications in footwear, automotive, and furniture lamination.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What solvents are used in neoprene contact adhesives and what are the regulatory concerns?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Traditional neoprene contact adhesives use blends of methyl ethyl ketone (MEK), toluene, acetone, and heptane. MEK provides fast evaporation and good polymer dissolution (OEL: 300 ppm); toluene is a reproductive toxicant (Category 2, EU CLP) with a 50 ppm STEL and is subject to strict occupational exposure controls. Both are regulated under EU Directive 2004/42/EC and the VOC Solvents Emissions Directive. These regulations, combined with REACH restrictions and occupational health pressure in shoe manufacturing, are driving the transition to water-based polychloroprene dispersions and solvent-free high-viscosity paste systems.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How does an SBS contact adhesive differ from a neoprene contact adhesive?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">SBS (styrene-butadiene-styrene) contact adhesives use a thermoplastic elastomer with hard polystyrene end blocks and a rubbery polybutadiene midblock. SBS does not undergo strain-induced crystallisation, so initial peel strength on contact is lower than neoprene. However, SBS remains flexible to lower temperatures (to approximately −20°C), is less expensive, and performs well for floor covering, construction lamination, and panel bonding where maximum immediate bond strength is less critical. SIS (styrene-isoprene-styrene) variants offer higher tack than SBS through the more flexible polyisoprene midblock but with lower heat resistance.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What surfaces can contact adhesives bond and what preparation is required?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Neoprene and SBS contact adhesives bond rubber, foam, leather, wood, particle board, HPL laminates, most rigid plastics, fabric, and metal. Low-energy surfaces (polyethylene, polypropylene, PTFE, silicone) require corona treatment, flame treatment, or specialist primer before application. Both surfaces must be clean, dry, and free from release agents, dust, and grease. Porous substrates require a single coat; non-porous surfaces require thin, uniform coats — heavy application on non-porous substrates traps solvent and reduces tack. Uniform application without missed areas and correct open time management are the two most critical application variables for bond strength.</div>
        </details>
      </section>

